[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "260726-gftd-jk-luxury-kotobase-postgres-policy"
  :adr/title "gftdcojp and jk-luxury products use Cloudflare D1 (SQLite-backed) Kotobase by default"
  :adr/status "active"
  :adr/doc_type "adr"
  :adr/topic "organization-database-policy"
  :adr/last_verified "2026-07-26"
  :adr/authoritative_for
  ["gftdcojp and jk-luxury application database defaults"
   "Kotobase API generation for new product persistence"
   "Cloudflare/D1 host and credential boundary"]
  :adr/body "# Decision\n\n**Correction (2026-07-26, same day): the original text of this ADR named PostgreSQL-backed Kotobase as the default, based on a mistaken belief that Cloudflare D1 is PostgreSQL-backed. D1 is SQLite-backed, not PostgreSQL. The owner has since clarified the actual intent: SQLite-backed (Cloudflare D1) Kotobase is the default, not Postgres-backed. This body has been rewritten in place to reflect that (root CLAUDE.md's docs-represent-current-state convention — this is a same-day correction of a misunderstanding, not a later policy reversal, so the body is corrected directly rather than superseded).**\n\nAll new persistence and material persistence migrations in the `gftdcojp` and `jk-luxury` organizations assume the Cloudflare-accessed **D1 (SQLite-backed)** Kotobase service as the durable database, by default. Product code targets the current `kotobase.core` Datomic-shaped API: `open`, `transact!`, `datoms`, `q`, `query`, and `pull`. The default provider layer is `kotobase-storage-d1`; applications do not implement SQL repositories and do not create new dependencies on the legacy document/stream `kotobase.store/IStore` compatibility surface.\n\nPostgreSQL-backed Kotobase (`kotobase-storage-postgres`) remains available as an explicit, opt-in provider choice for products that need it (e.g. heavier multi-tenant pools, workloads that specifically require Postgres-only features) — see net-kotobase ADR-2607260930's Tier 2 (\"Managed Security Datomic\") framing, where storage-provider choice (D1 vs Postgres) is a per-tenant decision within the same tier, not a policy violation either way. What this ADR fixes is the **default when a product does not explicitly choose** — that default is now D1/SQLite, not Postgres.\n\n# Runtime boundary\n\nThe Cloudflare host owns D1 binding/transport construction, tenant/database/ref selection, CACAO authorization, encryption/decryption, blind indexing, visibility policy, pooling (D1's own connection model), migrations, and secrets. Product modules receive an already-open database or an authenticated database API binding. Secrets and direct D1 binding configuration never enter product records, browser bundles, or repository configuration.\n\n# Tenant policy\n\nEach organization and product receives an explicit tenant/database/ref namespace. Cross-organization reads are denied by default and require an auditable capability. Recommended ref names are `<org>/<repo>/<environment>`. Immutable blocks may deduplicate by CID, but mutable refs and authorization remain tenant-scoped.\n\n# Development and migration\n\nIn-memory storage is allowed only as a deterministic test oracle. Production must fail closed when Kotobase injection is absent; silent local-storage fallback (bypassing Kotobase entirely) is prohibited — this is the actual failure mode this policy guards against, independent of which Kotobase storage provider (D1 or Postgres) backs it. Existing D1, KV, direct PostgreSQL, and legacy IStore applications migrate incrementally when materially changed, with dual-read or verified backfill where required. This policy does not authorize destructive deletion of an old source before parity and rollback evidence exist."}]
